CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

The implementation of protection of women and children victims of violence is carried out based on:
a. humanity;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. protection;
d. non-discrimination;
e. respect and recognition of human rights;
f. best interests of women and children; and
g. victim protection.

Article 4

Forms of violence against women and children are:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence; or
d. household neglect.

Article 5

Physical violence as referred to in Article 4 letter a includes acts that result in:
a. pain according to medical experts;
b. injury;
c. wounds or disability to a person's body;
d. miscarriage;
e. fainting; and/or
f. causing death.

Article 6

Psychological violence as referred to in Article 4 letter b includes acts that among others result in:
a. fear;
b. loss of self-confidence;
c. loss of ability to act; and/or
d. feeling of helplessness.

Article 8

1. Household neglect as referred to in Article 4 letter d is an act of neglecting a person within the household scope;
2. Neglect as referred to in paragraph (1) also applies to any person who causes economic dependence so that the victim is under the control of that person, by:
a. restricting and/or prohibiting from working properly inside or outside the home;
b. not providing livelihood; or
c. eliminating access and control as well as participation regarding economic resources.

Article 9

1. The scope of acts of violence against women and children includes:a. acts of violence occurring within the household; and
b. acts of violence occurring outside the household.
2. Acts of violence occurring within the household as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a occur within the household committed by:
a. husband or wife within a marriage;
b. persons having blood relations, marriage relations, customary relations, adoption; orc. persons working for others or living and residing with others.
3. Acts of violence occurring outside the household as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b occur outside the household including:
a. harassment;
b. discriminatory violence in the workplace;
c. violence in conflict areas;
d. violence in mass media; and
e. trafficking in persons.

CHAPTER IV

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 10

Women and children victims of violence shall receive the following rights:
a. respect for their dignity as human beings;
b. recovery of health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced;
c. determination of their own decisions;
d. guarantee of their rights relating to their status as wife, mother or child and member of the household as well as member of the community;
e. compensation;
f. complaint handling;
g. obtaining protection, information, integrated services, continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage and confidential handling whether from individuals, groups or institutions whether government or non-government;
h. obtaining access and services at government and private health service facilities;
i. obtaining ease in the judicial process; and
j. psychological and legal assistance.

Article 11

Child victims of acts of violence, in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 10, shall also receive the following special rights:
a. respect and full use for survival, growth and development;
b. basic services especially 12 years compulsory education;
c. equal protection;
d. freedom from various stigma; and
e. obtaining freedom.

Article 13

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 12 letter a, include:
a. implementing policies for the protection of women and children from acts of violence established by the Government;
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
d. providing integrated services for women and children victims of acts of violence;
e. guaranteeing the implementation of protection for women and children victims of acts of violence by taking into account the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, husbands or other persons legally responsible for the victim;
f. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
g. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children victims of acts of violence according to regional financial capacity;
h. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children victims of acts of violence;
i. opening the widest possible access to information for women and children victims of acts of violence.
2. The implementation of obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be set forth in a Regional Action Plan containing programs and activities for the protection of women and children victims of acts of violence;
3. The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) shall become part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan to be subsequently used as a basis for SKPD in implementing protection of women and children victims of acts of violence;
4. Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be regulated in a Mayoral Regulation.

Article 14

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the community as referred to in Article 12 letter b shall be implemented in the form of community participation through:
a. prevention of acts of violence against women and children;
b. providing information and/or reporting acts of violence against women and children to the competent authorities;
c. assistance and aid in handling women and children victims of acts of violence;
d. efforts to protect and provide moral and/or material support to women and children victims of acts of violence.
2. Provisions regarding procedures for the implementation of community participation shall be regulated in a Mayoral Regulation.

CHAPTER VI

PREVENTION OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 16

1. To prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government shall conduct empowerment and awareness-raising to families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling;
2. In addition to empowerment and awareness-raising as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall conduct the following efforts:
a. increasing the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, and improvement of social services;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern for the prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women and children; and
g. opening complaint posts for the protection of women and children from acts of violence.

Article 18

1. Prevention of the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 16, shall be implemented by SKPD whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. women's empowerment and child protection;
f. mental and spiritual; and
g. peace and order.
2. Prevention of acts of violence by SKPD as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

Article 19

1. Forms of services provided to women and children victims of violence include:
a. health/medical services in the form of treatment and recovery of the victim's physical condition carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;
b. medico-legal services which are a form of medical services for evidentiary purposes in the legal field;
c. psychosocial services which are services provided by companions in order to recover the victim's traumatic condition, by providing a safe house to protect victims from various threats and intimidation;
d. provision of moral/mental support to restore self-confidence, mental strength and independence in facing and resolving the problems befalling them;
e. provision of legal services and assistance to help victims undergo legal processes related to the violence cases they face; and
f. economic independence services in the form of services for skills training and providing economic access so that victims can be independent.
2. Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the Government;
3. Provisions regarding procedures for services to women and children victims of violence shall be regulated in a Mayor Regulation.

Article 20

1. The Regional Government is obliged to provide complaint services by victims of violence;
2. The Regional Government must establish a recovery house or safe house;
3. Provisions regarding complaint services and social services as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be regulated in a Mayor Regulation.

Article 21

1. Services to victims of violence shall be carried out by service institutions established by the Regional Government as well as non-governmental institutions;
2. Provision of services by the Regional Government as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by regional apparatus having the principal tasks and functions of providing services in the fields of health and social welfare;
3. Provision of services other than by regional apparatus as referred to in paragraph (2) may also be carried out by police agencies;
4. Provision of services by non-governmental institutions as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by community organizations engaged in advocacy and services for women and children victims of violence.

Article 22

1. The Regional Government in providing protection services to women and children victims of violence through P2TP2A;
2. P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) functions as an integrated service center in providing protection to women and children from violence.

Article 23

1. Provision of services carried out by the Regional Government as well as non-governmental institutions includes receiving complaints and providing assistance;
2. In carrying out its duties, non-governmental service institutions as referred to in paragraph (1) shall coordinate and cooperate with P2TP2A established by the Regional Government.

Article 24

1. The Regional Government facilitates temporary protection, legal protection and support directly or channels to third parties, whether police, foundations, non-governmental organizations, or individuals for women and children victims of violence;
2. Protection and support as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out from the time a report is received, whether from the victim or other parties or from the time violence against women and children is known to have occurred.

Article 26

1. Medical services provided to victims include provision of visum et repertum, DNA examination, health examination, inpatient and outpatient care, as well as psychosocial and spiritual services;
2. Services organized by P2TP2A must be carried out quickly in accordance with the victim's needs;
3. Provision of medical services, counseling and reporting by P2TP2A shall be carried out free of charge.

Article 27

1. The Regional Government and or integrated service institutions for women and children victims of violence and community organizations, social organizations or non-governmental organizations are obliged to handle cases confidentially;
2. Confidential handling of cases of women and children victims of violence shall be carried out at the request of the applicant, companion and/or counselor;
3. Handling of cases of women and children victims of violence may be reported by the Regional Government and/or service institutions for women and children victims of violence, and community organizations, social organizations or non-governmental organizations if the victim and/or companion has given written permission.

Article 28

1. The Regional Government provides a safe house that is kept confidential with all its facilities, the financing of which is charged to the APBD;
2. Services at the safe house are provided to victims by fulfilling the following requirements:
a. filling out a form for self-identity and fulfillment of victim's needs;
b. signing an agreement on the confidentiality of the safe house's whereabouts; and
c. complying with the provisions in the safe house.
3. Services provided at the safe house include counseling, empowerment activities and victim independence;
4. Service facilities at the safe house are also provided for child victims according to their needs;
5. Further provisions regarding services at the safe house shall be stipulated in the Safe House Standard Operating Procedures.

Article 29

1. The Regional Government facilitates alternative housing that is kept confidential and needed by victims who are not accommodated in the safe house or victims who feel psychologically uncomfortable living in the safe house with all its facilities;
2. The victim's alternative housing must be clear and can be monitored by the Regional Government and companions.

CHAPTER VIII

REHABILITATION AND REINTEGRATION

Article 31

1. Every woman and child victim of violence has the right to obtain rehabilitation both physically and psychologically;
2. Rehabilitation facilitation services include counseling services, medical psychology, legal assistance and skills training or alternative education;
3. Every woman and child victim of violence who has recovered both physically and psychologically has the right to be integrated or returned to their family, community and educational institutions for those still in school status;
4. Fulfillment of the integration rights of women and children victims of violence shall be carried out through cooperation and coordination among all related SKPD, vertical agencies in the region, community organizations, non-governmental organizations and family parties;
5. Further provisions regarding the implementation of rehabilitation and reintegration shall be regulated in a Mayor Regulation.

Article 34

Treatment of children as perpetrators of criminal acts as referred to in Article 33 letter a, is as follows:
a. investigators undertake efforts to handle cases of children in conflict with the law with a restorative justice approach for the best interests of the child and must involve the Correctional Center, parents and/or families of victims and perpetrators of criminal acts as well as local community leaders;
b. the correctional center is obliged to prepare a correctional research report;
c. in the event that a child is detained, their placement is separated from adult detainees or placed in a detention house specifically for children;
d. the investigation process and submission of case files to the Public Prosecutor is carried out immediately by including the Community Supervisor of the Correctional Center;
e. in the event that the Public Prosecutor is of the opinion that from the results of the investigation and the results of community research prosecution can be carried out by means of a restorative justice approach, then the Public Prosecutor immediately submits the case file to the District Court;
f. after receiving the submission from the Public Prosecutor, the Judge immediately conducts a child trial with a restorative justice approach procedure;
g. if the court decision is in the form of an action, then the Correctional Center is obliged to conduct guidance and supervision;
h. guidance, coaching and care at the Correctional Center, Detention House and Correctional Institution is carried out in an integrated manner involving relevant agencies; and
i. in the event that the court decision hands over the child in conflict with the law to the Social Service, then the Social Service is obliged to receive and prepare facilities and infrastructure in the context of social recovery and rehabilitation of the child.

Article 35

Treatment of children as victims of criminal acts as referred to in Article 33 letter b, is as follows:
a. immediately after receiving a report from the victim of a criminal act, the investigator conducts an initial interview;
b. in conducting the interview, the investigator pays attention to the situation and condition of the victim;
c. if the victim requires further treatment, after the initial interview the victim may be treated or placed in a special (temporary) room for other required services such as medical care, psychological care or examination in the context of the investigation and inquiry process; and
d. after the victim recovers and all services including the legal process are completed, the victim may be returned home or placed in a child social protection house, safe house, trauma center, for social and mental rehabilitation.

Article 36

Treatment of children as witnesses to criminal acts as referred to in Article 33 letter c, is as follows:
a. in the event that a child reports as a witness, then the police immediately contact the child's parents or guardian, unless the child's parents or guardian are involved or suspected as perpetrators;
b. make a record of the identity of the referring party, data regarding the child, chronology of events;
c. request the parent or guardian trusted by the child, to accompany the child when the child provides testimony;
d. in the event that a child provides testimony in the investigation process, it is conducted in a special and closed room accompanied by parents/guardian taking into account the age, intellectual level, and importance of the child's testimony;
e. the child has the right to receive protection from witness and victim protection institutions in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
f. if medical treatment is required, the child is referred to the nearest hospital;
g. at every stage of examination up to examination in court, the child as a witness must be provided with an assistant.

Article 37

1. In order to achieve the objectives of protecting women and children from acts of violence as referred to in Article 3, the Regional Government cooperates with:
a. the Government;
b. the Provincial Government;
c. other Regency/City Governments; and/or
d. non-governmental institutions.
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) is followed up in accordance with applicable law, including:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. provision of evidence and witnesses;
c. rehabilitation of victims of acts of violence; and
d. repatriation and social reintegration.
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) is set forth in the form of a Joint Agreement.

Article 38

1. The Regional Government establishes partnerships with the business world in the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
2. Partnership as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out through:
a. notification of information on employment opportunities for women victims of acts of violence;
b. education and training for women victims of acts of violence;
c. educational assistance for children victims of acts of violence;
d. fostering and increasing the economic independence of women victims of acts of violence.
4. Partnership as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) is set forth in the form of an agreement.

CHAPTER XIII

GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

Article 40

1. The Regional Government is obliged to conduct guidance and supervision of the implementation of protection of women and children victims of acts of violence;
2. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. service guidelines and standards;
b. technical guidance and training;
c. provision of facilities;
d. monitoring; and
e. evaluation.
3. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (2) is in order to realize the objectives of protection of women and children victims of acts of violence in accordance with minimum service standards implemented by SKPD/P2TP2A and the community.

Article 41

1. The Regional Government is obliged to conduct supervision of the implementation of protection of women and children victims of acts of violence;
2. Supervision as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out with the principles of professionalism, transparency and accountability.
